      "text": {
        "title": "Wem und was?",
        "de": "Der Arzt gibt dem Patienten die Tablette. Die Pflegekraft zeigt der Patientin das Zimmer. Die Tochter kauft dem Sohn einen Ball. Die Person steht im Dativ. Die Sache steht im Akkusativ.",
        "en": "The doctor gives the patient the tablet. The nurse shows the patient the room. The daughter buys the son a ball. The person is in the dative. The thing is in the accusative."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar",
      "type": "grammar",
      "label": "Grammar",
      "title": "Wem and was",
      "practice": "choose",
      "heroTitle": "geben, zeigen, erklären",
      "heroImage": "tablet",
      "story": [
        "Some verbs take two objects. <b>geben, zeigen, erklären, bringen, reichen, schicken, kaufen</b> are the ones on this ward.",
        "The person is dative. <b>Der Arzt gibt dem Patienten die Tablette.</b> Ask <b>Wem?</b>",
        "The thing is accusative. Ask <b>Was?</b> Feminine dative is <b>der</b>, not die: <b>der Patientin</b>.",
        "Say the person before the thing when both are nouns. <b>helfen</b> is different: only the person, no thing. That is the next chapter."
      ],
      "questionRule": "Wem? for the person. Was? for the thing. Masculine dative dem, feminine dative der, neuter dative dem. Masculine accusative den or einen.",
      "tables": [
        {
          "title": "Dativ der Person",
          "caption": "The receiver. Feminine dative is der.",
